Make an HTTP request to any URL. Use for REST APIs, JSON endpoints, anything that doesn't need a browser. For browser-rendered pages or stateful sessions, use `playwright_manager` instead.

**Pod-aware auth.** Header values may be `datapod:` references — sealed credentials (Bearer tokens, API keys) that the tool resolves at courier scope. The resolved string never enters your transcript. Format: `datapod:<kind>:<id>` (implicit 'full' projection) or `datapod:<kind>:<id>/<projection>` (explicit).

**Pod-aware body.** The entire body can come from a pod via the same `datapod:` syntax — useful for file uploads (image / PDF bytes from `mint_pod_from_path`) or sensitive submissions (SSN/PII you should not see). Per-field pod substitution inside a dict body is NOT supported; construct a single pod up front.

**Pod-sealed response.** Set `response_pod_kind` (e.g. `'health.private'`, `'financial.private'`) to seal the response body into a NEW pod. The tool returns `response_pod_id` instead of the body content; a downstream skill at the appropriate authority can `pod_fetch` to act on it. The agent driving this call never reads the response.

When NOT to use:
- Static page reads that don't need an API → `scrape_url`
- Browser-rendered SPAs / login flows → `playwright_manager`
- Calling another agent / running a routine → those are managers, not HTTP

Always pass `expect_status` if you want the call to fail loudly on non-2xx responses (default lets the agent see the status and decide).
